\section{Data carried per region}{

The leaftable holds two families of per-region data, and the distinction
between them decides what each column can be used for.

\strong{Geographic}, from the region shapes, defined for all 1,477 atoms:\tabular{lll}{
   column \tab unit \tab kind \cr
   \code{km2} \tab km2, EPSG:3035 \tab extensive \cr
   \code{pop} \tab thousands \tab extensive \cr
   \code{gdp} \tab EUR per capita \tab \strong{intensive} \cr
   \code{gdp_total} \tab thousand EUR \tab extensive \cr
}


\strong{Model}, from the NUTS3 network, located at substation buses:\tabular{lll}{
   column \tab unit \tab kind \cr
   \code{load_twh} \tab TWh per year \tab extensive \cr
   \code{peak_mw} \tab MW, coincident \tab \strong{not additive} \cr
   \code{n_buses} \tab count of substations \tab extensive \cr
   \code{cap_mw} \tab MW of generation built today \tab extensive \cr
   \code{hydro_mw} \tab MW of that which is hydro \tab extensive \cr
   \code{pot_onwind}, \code{pot_solar}, \code{pot_solar_hsat} \tab MW \tab extensive \cr
   \code{pot_offwind_ac}, \code{pot_offwind_dc}, \code{pot_offwind_float} \tab MW \tab extensive \cr
}


\code{gdp} looks like a total and is not: summing it gives Germany "15.6
million", while a population-weighted mean recovers its real ~41,800 EUR per
capita. \code{gdp_total = gdp * pop} is carried so an extensive money weight
exists. \code{peak_mw} is the maximum of the \emph{summed} hourly series and falls
below the sum of its parts wherever demand is diverse; see \link{nuts_load}.

Seven columns are declared \strong{weights} -- \code{km2}, \code{pop}, \code{gdp_total},
\code{load_twh}, \code{cap_mw}, \code{pot_onwind}, \code{pot_solar} -- and \code{km2} is the default.
A weight splits a coarse quantity across atoms and averages an intensive one
back up, so the choice is a modelling decision: national demand should be
split by \code{load_twh} or \code{pop}, not by area, and a wind target by \code{pot_onwind}.
See \code{\link[geoscales:recast_geoscale]{geoscales::recast_geoscale()}}.
}

\section{The potentials overlap and must not be summed}{

\code{pot_solar} and \code{pot_solar_hsat} are fixed-tilt and single-axis-tracking
photovoltaics \strong{on the same land}, and 313 of the 314 offshore regions carry
more than one \verb{pot_offwind_*} type on the same sea area. Adding them counts
the same hectare twice. They are kept as separate columns so that no summing
rule is imposed here; the three offshore columns are excluded from the weight
set for a second reason -- they are zero for every landlocked country.
}

\section{442 regions carry no model data}{

PyPSA-Eur places demand, plant and potential at substation buses, and 442 of
the 1,477 NUTS3 regions contain none. Every model column is zero there. That
is the model's own allocation -- a busless region's land and load were folded
into the neighbour it clusters with -- and not a statement about the region.
The geographic columns are unaffected.

The effect propagates upward exactly as far as the node counts do: \strong{7 NUTS2
groups and 3 NUTS1 groups} contain no substation at all, and these are
precisely the regions lost when 296 NUTS2 become 289 nodes and 109 NUTS1
become 106.

A weight summing to zero over a group divides by zero when averaging an
intensive quantity into it. Those groups are recorded rather than patched:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{attr(nuts_gs, "weight_degeneracy")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Two entries are degenerate for a reason other than a missing substation, and
both are real: \code{GBI3} (Inner London) has no generation and no land the
availability rules admit for wind, and \code{NO07} (Nord-Norge) has no solar
potential, lying above the Arctic Circle.
}

\section{Four countries have no NUTS}{

\strong{BA, MD, UA and XK} are outside the NUTS system entirely -- the same four
PyPSA-Eur caps at administrative level 1. They are filled from OpenStreetMap
\code{adm1} boundaries (3, 37, 27 and 7 regions respectively), which become
\code{nuts3} atoms, and the levels above them are \strong{padded}: \code{MD0} at \code{nuts1},
\code{MD00} at \code{nuts2}.

Padding rather than repeating matters. PyPSA-Eur repeats the adm1 code at
every level, which makes each of those regions \strong{its own parent}: the
derived region hierarchy then carries identity pairs like \verb{(MD-BA, MD-BA)},
and energyRt's spatial roll-up degenerates to
\code{vOutTot[MD-BA] = ... + vOutTot[MD-BA]}, silently forcing everything else to
zero.

The padding follows Eurostat's own convention for identical geography --
Luxembourg is \code{LU -> LU0 -> LU00 -> LU000} -- and no NUTS country produces a
single identity pair. The honest caveat remains that "NUTS1"/"NUTS2" for
those four is a stand-in, not a real administrative level.
}

\section{A known gap in the gdp weight}{

One region, \code{MD-BD} (Bender), carries \code{gdp = 0} in the source, and it is left
uncorrected -- substituting a plausible number would hide a real gap.

It no longer breaks anything: with the levels padded, \code{MD-BD} sits inside
\code{MD0}/\code{MD00} rather than being its own parent, so no group sums to zero and
every weight now passes the build script's checks cleanly.
}

\section{The geometry is simplified}{

Reduced from 37 MB of source GeoJSON with
\code{rmapshaper::ms_simplify(keep = 0.02, keep_shapes = TRUE)}; the build refuses
to save if a region is lost. It is a \strong{display} geometry -- the \code{km2} weight
was computed from the \emph{unsimplified} polygons in EPSG:3035, so use that
rather than measuring the shipped shapes.
}

